\"alternatives\": null,\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"maximum_capacity\": {\"value\": 8, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"},\n    \"used_in\": {\"value\": \"PMA-EV (Class II)\"}\n  }\n},\n{\n  \"role\": \"load_cell\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"Bizerba WS3\"],\n  \"alternatives\": null,\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"maximum_capacity\": {\"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"},\n    \"used_in\": {\"value\": \"PMA-HD (Class III)\"}\n  }\n}\n```\n\nShould alternatives be \"OR\"? They're not alternatives on the same instrument; different types use different cells. alternatives: null for each. Good.\n\nAlso Ex-link converter is a component for X/Y models: \"EX-link converter for voltage supply and data transfer (models EVO1X*, LAB1X*, VIS1X*, VIS1Y*)\". I can add component role \"Ex-link converter\" without type designation. Type_designations [] then. Maybe include with empty array and characteristics note. Eh, I'll include it: role: \"EX-link converter\", type_designations: [], characteristics: {\"applies_to\": ...}. Hmm, type_designations empty might violate \"don't fabricate\"? It's just empty. I'll include since it's a named sub-assembly. Actually components[] should have type_designations[]; empty array acceptable. I'll include it for completeness.\n\nNow characteristics.model_level. Attributes:\n\n1. accuracy_class: values \"II\" for 28 EV models, \"III\" for 10 HD models. (string values)\n2. maximum_capacity: 7500 g ×28, 2200 g ×10.\n3. minimum_capacity: 5 g ×28, 20 g ×10.\n4. actual_scale_interval: 0.1 g ×28, 1 g ×10.\n5. operating_temperature: {min:10, max:30} °C ×28; {min:10, max:40} °C ×10.\n6. load_cell_model: \"Bizerba WS8\" ×28; \"Bizerba WS3\" ×10.\n7. display_type: \"LCD segment display with fixed position touch keys\" for EVO1*, LAB1*, SPO1* (models: all EVO1 (12), all LAB1 (8), all SPO1 (2) = 22) and \"LCD graphic display with touch function\" for VIS1* (16). Wait — SPO1 models: SPO1S1N3CI-C, SPO1S1CI-C (2). LAB1X1..X4 with CI-C variants = 8. EVO1: S1(2) + Y1(2) + X1-X4 (8 each ×2 = 8? Let me count: EVO1X1N3CI-C, EVO1X1CI-C, EVO1X2N3CI-C, EVO1X2CI-C, EVO1X3N3CI-C, EVO1X3CI-C, EVO1X4N3CI-C, EVO1X4CI-C = 8). So EVO1 total = 2+2+8 = 12. EV total = 12 + 16 VIS = 28. ✓. HD = 2 + 8 = 10 ✓.\n\ndisplay_type: segment for EVO1 (12), LAB1 (8), SPO1 (2) = 22 models; graphic for VIS1 (16).\n\nShould I include display_type in model_level? It's a per-model attribute that varies. Yes, adds value. But it's verbose. I'll include it.\n\nVerification interval e = 1 g for both → type_level: {\"verification_scale_interval\": {\"value\": 1, \"unit_symbol\": \"g\"}}.\n\nTemperature: the table lists \"+10 to +30 °C\" and \"+10 to +40 °C\". Note: values min 10 max 30; min 10 max 40.\n\nHmm wait — is temperature usually -10 to +40? The cert says +10 to +30 °C (Class II) and +10 to +40 °C (Class III). Take as given.\n\nNow writing 38 entries per attribute × 6 attributes ≈ 228 value objects. JSON size will be large but fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I can reduce by using model lists per value? The schema fixed: values[]: {model, value}. Each value object references one model. To conform, I need 38 per attribute. OK, let's do it. Actually, hmm, alternatively I could interpret the \"model\" axis at the TYPE level (PMA-EV vs PMA-HD) since attributes co-vary. But the certificate explicitly lists 38 models, and rule 4's spirit is to capture per-model variation. However, models in model_family.models have attributes{} — maybe I can put all attributes there and keep model_level lean?\n\nRe-reading the schema:\n\n```\nmodel_family:\n  family_name\n  models[]:\n    model_id, label, attributes{}\n```\n\nattributes{} is freeform. So I could put {instrument_type, accuracy_class, max, min, d, temperature...} in each model's attributes. And model_level is under characteristics. The rules say \"Detect this and put varying attributes in model_level, NOT type_level.\" It doesn't mandate models[].attributes usage.\n\nI think the cleanest, most useful: model_family.models with minimal attributes ({instrument_type, accuracy_class}) and characteristics.model_level with full matrices. Duplication between the two is by design in the schema (the R137 example duplicates qmax etc. in both matrix_tables and model_level).\n\nAlternatively, to keep response size manageable, put all varying data in model_level and keep models[].attributes minimal. Yes.\n\nLet me also handle matrix_tables: Should the technical data table (Type PMA-EV/PMA-HD) be a matrix_table? It's a 2-D table: rows = attributes, columns = types. It fits model_level better (with model = type?). Hmm. matrix_tables is for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". This table fits model_level (attribute × model/type). But model_level values keyed by 38 models vs 2 types... The table's axis is type, not individual model. I'll capture the table as matrix_tables too? It says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". I'm putting it in model_level, so it fits. I could still add matrix_tables for fidelity with rows as attributes:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical data\",\n  \"columns\": [\"attribute\", \"PMA-EV\", \"PMA-HD\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"attribute\": \"class\", \"PMA-EV\": \"II\", \"PMA-HD\": \"III\"},\n    {\"attribute\": \"max\", \"PMA-EV\": \"7500 g\", ...},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis transposed form is awkward. I'll skip matrix_tables since model_level covers it. Hmm, but maybe include it for completeness with values as raw strings? I think model_level suffices; matrix_tables \"don't fit elsewhere\" — this fits in model_level. Skip.\n\nAlso the software table could be matrix? No, captured in components.\n\nNow certificate.page_total: 5. member_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\" — I might shorten to \"United Kingdom\". The doc says \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\". Use that full string? It's fine.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMO\" (National Measurement Office? The footer says \"NMO is part of the Office for Product Safety and Standards within the Department for Business, Energy & Industrial Strategy\". Name as printed: NMO.)\n- address_lines: [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Mannie Panesar\"\n- person_title: \"Head of Technical Services\"\n- phone: \"+44 (0) 20 8943 7272\"\n- website: \"www.gov.uk/government/organisations/office-for-product-safety-and-standards\"\n- email: not present → omit.\n\nThe signature block mentions M Bokota, Technical Manager — that's the signer, not person responsible. Keep Mannie Panesar as person responsible per the \"Person responsible:\" field.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\"\n- type_designations: all 38 model names. Also \"PMA-EV\" and \"PMA-HD\" as type names. Should type_designations include PMA-EV/PMA-HD? They're the instrument \"types\". The models are listed under certified type. I'll include the 38 model names in type_designations, and PMA-EV/PMA-HD in description/model family. Hmm — certified_type.type_designations[]: \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". So include all 38 plus PMA-EV, PMA-HD? PMA-EV/PMA-HD are type identifiers too. I'll include them: [\"PMA-EV\", \"PMA-HD\", then all models...]. Order: maybe list PMA-EV and PMA-HD first.\n\n- module_designation: For R76, module designation isn't typical. Omit or null. I'll set null? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit.\n\n- description: combine.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null? Or \"PMA\"? The text: \"The family comprises of the PMA-EV (Class II) and the PMA-HD (Class III) types\". I'll set family_name: null and let models carry type labels. Hmm, actually maybe family_name \"PMA\" is defensible. I'll go with null to avoid fabrication... Actually you know, the two \"types\" PMA-EV and PMA-HD are labeled; the family itself has no name. null it is.\n\nmodels[]: 38 entries:\n{\"model_id\": \"EVO1S1N3CI-C\", \"label\": \"PMA-EV\", \"attributes\": {\"instrument_type\": \"PMA-EV\", \"accuracy_class\": \"II\"}}\nlabel — what's label for? Perhaps display name. I'll set label to the instrument type? Or omit label. I'll set label: \"PMA-EV (paint mixing, Class II)\"? Simpler: label: \"Type PMA-EV\". Hmm. I'll use label to indicate the type group: \"PMA-EV\". And attributes with accuracy_class and environment? Let me include environment attributes since the naming encodes them:\n\n- A letter: S = standard, X = Ex-zone 1, Y = Ex-zone 2. Wait: \"A = S, X, or Y, denoting operation in: standard environments, Ex-zone 1 explosive environments, or Ex-zone 2 explosive environments respectively.\" So S→standard, X→zone 1, Y→zone 2.\n- B: for A=S and EVO1Y models, B=1; for all other models B ∈ 1,2,3,4 = RJ-45 cable length: no cable, 10m, 20m, 30m.\n\nInteresting: so for EVO1X models, B varies 1-4 → cable length varies. For VIS1, only X models have B 1-4? VIS1Y1M3CI-C has M3 — hmm, VIS1Y models have \"M3\" suffix. Wait: VIS1Y1M3CI-C — is B=1 with M3? The pattern VIS1 ABC → A=Y, B=1, and \"M3\" is part of C (commercial name affixes)? \"C is a placeholder for commercial name affixes.\" So M3 might be commercial affix. Hmm, actually for VIS1Y models: VIS1Y1M3CI-C, VIS1Y2M3CI-C, VIS1Y3M3CI-C, VIS1Y4M3CI-C — B = 1..4. And VIS1X models: B = 1..4. But the rule says \"For A=S and EVO1Y models, B = 1\" — so VIS1Y models DO have B 1-4 (only EVO1Y restricted to B=1). OK consistent with listings.\n\nI won't compute per-model cable lengths; too much inference. But environment per model is straightforward from the second letter:\n- EVO1S*: standard; EVO1Y*: Ex zone 2; EVO1X*: Ex zone 1.\n- VIS1Y*: zone 2; VIS1X*: zone 1.\n- SPO1S*: standard; LAB1X*: zone 1.\n\nI could add environment attribute per model. That's genuinely useful and derivable directly from stated naming rule. I'll include \"environment\" in attributes. And accuracy_class. And instrument_type.\n\nSo models[] entries like:\n{\"model_id\": \"EVO1S1N3CI-C\", \"label\": \"PMA-EV\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": \"II\", \"environment\": \"standard\"}}\n{\"model_id\": \"EVO1X1N3CI-C\", ..., \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": \"II\", \"environment\": \"Ex-zone 1\"}}\n\nAlso maybe cable variants noted: models with B>1 have RJ-45 cable 10/20/30m. E.g., EVO1X3N3CI-C → B=3 → 20m. Let me derive: B=1 → no cable; 2 → 10m; 3 → 20m; 4 → 30m. Should I add \"rj45_cable_length\" attribute? It's directly stated (\"B = 1, 2, 3, or 4 depending on supplied RJ-45 cable lengths of: no cable, 10m, 20m, or 30m respectively\"). Deriving per-model is deterministic. It adds value but also risk of clutter. Hmm. For A=S and EVO1Y models B=1 (no cable). For others B varies per model number.\n\nE.g. EVO1X2N3CI-C → B=2 → 10 m. VIS1Y3M3CI-C → B=3 → 20 m. I could include \"rj45_cable_length\": {\"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"} or \"none\". Let me include it — deterministic from explicit rule. Actually, wait: is it worth the complexity? It adds 38 attribute entries. Since I'm already writing 38 models, adding an attribute per model is cheap (one line each). I'll include.\n\nFor S models: cable none. EVO1Y: none. EVO1X2: 10m. etc.\n\nLet me enumerate all 38 with attributes:\n\nPMA-EV (Class II):\n1. EVO1S1N3CI-C — env standard, cable none\n2. EVO1S1CI-C — standard, none\n3. EVO1Y1N3CI-C — zone 2, none\n4. EVO1Y1CI-C — zone 2, none\n5. EVO1X1N3CI-C — zone 1, none (B=1)\n6. EVO1X1CI-C — zone 1, none\n7. EVO1X2N3CI-C — zone 1, 10 m\n8. EVO1X2CI-C — zone 1, 10 m\n9. EVO1X3N3CI-C — zone 1, 20 m\n10. EVO1X3CI-C — zone 1, 20 m\n11. EVO1X4N3CI-C — zone 1, 30 m\n12. EVO1X4CI-C — zone 1, 30 m\n13. VIS1Y1M3CI-C — zone 2, none\n14. VIS1Y1CI-C — zone 2, none\n15. VIS1Y2M3CI-C — zone 2, 10 m\n16. VIS1Y2CI-C — zone 2, 10 m\n17. VIS1Y3M3CI-C — zone 2, 20 m\n18. VIS1Y3CI-C — zone 2, 20 m\n19. VIS1Y4M3CI-C — zone 2, 30 m\n20. VIS1Y4CI-C — zone 2, 30 m\n21. VIS1X1M3CI-C — zone 1, none\n22. VIS1X1CI-C — zone 1, none\n23. VIS1X2M3CI-C — zone 1, 10 m\n24. VIS1X2CI-C — zone 1, 10 m\n25. VIS1X3M3CI-C — zone 1, 20 m\n26. VIS1X3CI-C — zone 1, 20 m\n27. VIS1X4M3CI-C — zone 1, 30 m\n28. VIS1X4CI-C — zone 1, 30 m\n\nPMA-HD (Class III):\n29. SPO1S1N3CI-C — standard, none\n30. SPO1S1CI-C — standard, none\n31. LAB1X1N3CI-C — zone 1, none\n32. LAB1X1CI-C — zone 1, none\n33. LAB1X2N3CI-C — zone 1, 10 m\n34. LAB1X2CI-C — zone 1, 10 m\n35. LAB1X3N3CI-C — zone 1, 20 m\n36. LAB1X3CI-C — zone 1, 20 m\n37. LAB1X4N3CI-C — zone 1, 30 m\n38. LAB1X4CI-C — zone 1, 30 m\n\nHmm wait, for VIS1Y models B=1..4 present (VIS1Y1M3, VIS1Y2M3...). The text said only \"EVO1Y models\" have B=1 forced. VIS1Y2M3CI-C → B=2 → 10 m. Yes as I did.\n\nWait, but actually \"For A =S and EVO1Y models, B = 1 and for all other models B = 1, 2, 3, or 4\". So VIS1Y B varies. ✓.\n\nAlso note M3 in VIS1Y/VIS1X models — part of commercial name affix C. Not interpreting.\n\nHmm, should I really include cable length in attributes? The concern: \"Don't fabricate\" — this is derived from explicit rules, not fabricated. I'll include it as \"rj45_cable_length\" with value \"none\" or numeric meters. Structured values required for characteristics; models[].attributes{} is freeform, but I'll use consistent {value, unit_symbol} objects. Let me use structured there too: \"rj45_cable_length\": {\"value\": \"none\"} or {\"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"}. Good.\n\nNow model_level — given I'm putting accuracy_class, environment, cable in model attributes, the model_level matrix should carry the quantitative technical data: maximum_capacity, minimum_capacity, actual_scale_interval, operating_temperature, load_cell_model, display_type, verification_scale_interval? e is same for all (1 g) → type_level.\n\nLet me decide final model_level attributes:\n1. maximum_capacity (g): 7500 / 2200\n2. minimum_capacity (g): 5 / 20\n3. actual_scale_interval (g): 0.1 / 1\n4. operating_temperature_range (°C): {10,30} / {10,40}\n5. load_cell: \"Bizerba WS8\" / \"Bizerba WS3\" (string values)\n6. display_type: segment / graphic (string)\n7. accuracy_class: \"II\"/\"III\"? Also in model attributes. Include here too? To be safe for matrix consumers, include accuracy_class in model_level as well. Hmm, duplication. The schema example put qmax in model_level and also in matrix_tables. models[].attributes{} was in the R60 example minimal (just model_id). I'll include accuracy_class in both models[].attributes and model_level. It's cheap-ish: 38 short entries. Actually you know what, to control size, I'll put accuracy_class ONLY in models[].attributes and NOT in model_level? But then consumers reading model_level miss class... The R76 example in my head: rules say varying attributes → model_level. Accuracy class varies. I'll include it in model_level too. Fine, verbose but complete.\n\nActually, let me reconsider size: 7 attributes × 38 = 266 value objects each ~4 lines → ~1000+ lines JSON. That's very long. Hmm. Is there a smarter representation? I could group values by... no, schema is fixed: values[] {model, value}.\n\nOptions to reduce: reduce model_level attributes to the quantitative ones (max, min, d, temp, load_cell) and skip display_type/accuracy_class (put display_type in description, accuracy_class in models[].attributes). That gives 5 × 38 = 190 entries. Still long.\n\nAlternatively: treat \"model\" axis as the two types PMA-EV / PMA-HD in model_level, and keep models[] for the 38 names. But that mixes axes.\n\nHonestly, thoroughness is preferred per the system prompt (\"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule). I'll write it out fully with model ids. To keep line count lower I can put each value object on one line: {\"model\": \"EVO1S1N3CI-C\", \"value\": 7500, \"unit_symbol\": \"g\", \"unit_id\": null}. That's compact-ish. Actually the schema shows unit_symbol/unit_id optional? The example: {\"model\": \"Emax=25t\", \"value\": 25} — no unit_symbol. Rule 3 says every characteristic value MUST be object with that shape (value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers). The R60 example omitted unit_symbol in values (unit at attribute level: \"unit_symbol\": \"t\"). I'll follow the example: attribute-level unit_symbol, value objects with model+value (+footnote_markers only when present). That keeps objects small: {\"model\": \"X\", \"value\": 7500}.\n\nFor string values like load_cell, unit_symbol null.\n\nLet me draft the model_level:\n\n```json\n\"model_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"accuracy_class\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": null,\n    \"values\": [ {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": \"II\"} ×28, {...\"III\"} ×10 ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_capacity\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"g\",\n    \"values\": [ ... 7500×28, 2200×10 ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"minimum_capacity\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"g\",\n    \"values\": [ 5×28, 20×10 ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"actual_scale_interval\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"g\",\n    \"values\": [ 0.1×28, 1×10 ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"operating_temperature_range\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\",\n    \"values\": [ {min:10,max:30}×28, {min:10,max:40}×10 ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"load_cell\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": null,\n    \"values\": [ \"Bizerba WS8\"×28, \"Bizerba WS3\"×10 ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"display_type\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": null,\n    \"values\": [ segment×22 (EVO1,LPO? no—EVO1 12 + LAB1 8 + SPO1 2 = 22), graphic×16 ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nOrder of models: I'll keep the certificate's listed order.\n\nType level:\n```json\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"verification_scale_interval\": {\"value\": 1, \"unit_symbol\": \"g\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n  \"maximum_tare\": {\"value\": \"Max\", ...}  // T ≤ -Max\n  \"initial_zero_setting_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 20}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n  \"initial_zero_setting_reference\": {\"value\": \"Max\"},\n  \"zero_tracking_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n  \"zero_tracking_reference\": {\"value\": \"Max\"},\n  \"power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n  \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"DC\"},\n  \"software...\"? \n}\n```\n\nFor zero tracking: \"Zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max)\". Hmm, actually R76 says zero tracking ≤ 0.5 e/s and range ±... The \"4% of Max\"? Hmm wait, initial zero setting ≤ 20% of Max is standard. Zero tracking 4%? Hmm the doc says \"(≤ 4% of Max)\"? Let me re-read: \"– Zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max)\". Hmm, unusual but as printed. Hmm, maybe it's 4% — I'll take as printed.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me re-read the markdown: \"Initial zero setting device (≤ 20% of Max)\", \"Zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max)\". Hmm, hold on, let me check: it says \"≤ 4% of Max\"? The text: `–&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max)`. Hmm, but common is 4% of Max for initial zeroing... no matter, take as printed.\n\nPower: 5 V DC from USB.\n\nScale interval naming: e = verification scale interval → \"verification_scale_interval\"; d = actual scale interval → \"actual_scale_interval\". Max → maximum_capacity; Min → minimum_capacity.\n\nNow also the \"T ≤ - Max\" — attribute \"maximum_tare\" with value \"Max\"? or \"-Max\"? Hmm. In table: `<tr><td>T ≤</td><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">- Max</td></tr>`. I'll record: {\"maximum_tare_effect\": {\"value\": \"-Max\", \"unit_symbol\": null}} — hmm. Or name \"maximum_tare\" value \"−Max\". I'll use value \"-Max\" and unit null. Actually maybe better: value \"Max\" with note that tare ≤ Max... I'll just use \"-Max\" as faithful string. Eh. Let me use \"maximum_tare\": {\"value\": \"-Max\"}. Fine.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R76\", edition 2006, amendment null, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [\"II\",\"III\"].\n\nTest reports:\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\": \"P02276\", \"date\": \"2018-05-11\", \"pages\": 30, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"P02276-D\", \"date\": \"2018-05-11\", \"pages\": null, \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n```\n\nRevision history:\n```json\n[{\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": 