— string\n- \"maximum_tare\" — type-level, value \"-Max\"\n\nType-level also:\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {min:null,max:20} %\n- initial_zero_setting_reference: \"Max\"\n- zero_tracking_range: {min:null,max:4} %\n- zero_tracking_reference: \"Max\"\n- power_supply_voltage: 5 V\n- power_supply_type: \"DC\"\n\nHmm \"power_supply_type\": value \"DC\" — string fine.\n\nAlso \"interfaces\" — description.\n\nOne more consideration: rule 2 requires every characteristic value to be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. For type_level I'll include unit_id: null always, footnote_markers only when present (none here). For string values unit_symbol null. OK.\n\nFor model_level values: example format omits unit_id. I'll use {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...} with unit at attribute level; for string-valued attributes set unit_symbol null.\n\nNow, assemble.\n\nAlso — should I add matrix_tables for the technical data? I decided no. But hmm, the schema says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". The Type table fits model_level. Software table fits components. Skip matrix_tables.\n\nWait, also should I record \"level indicator\", \"3 feet 2 adjustable\" — construction details in description. Yes.\n\nLet me also double check the temperature unit extraction: \"+10 to +30 °C\" → {min: 10, max: 30}, unit °C. Good.\n\nLoad cell Emax 8 kg / 3 kg — in components characteristics as maximum_capacity. Also maybe model_level \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\"? I'll keep in components only; model_level has \"load_cell\" designation. Hmm, actually the Emax per model could be useful in model_level too... it co-varies with load cell. Keep in components to limit size.\n\nNow recommendation:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"R76\",\n  \"edition\": 2006,\n  \"amendment\": null,\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"accuracy_classes\": [\"II\", \"III\"]\n}\n```\nOmit amendment (not present). I'll omit rather than null. Accuracy classes II and III.\n\ntest_reports as above. pages: 30 for the TER. For documentation file, pages not stated → omit pages.\n\nrevision_history: [{\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2018-05-11\", \"changes\": \"OIML Certificate first issued.\"}]\n\nfootnotes: omit.\n\nNow — the certificate \"number\": \"R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07\". Keep as-is.\n\nmember_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2018-05-11\".\n\npage_total: 5.\n\nLet me also add \"oiml_issuer_id\" — omit.\n\nDouble-check applicants address: \"37070 Goettingen, Germany\". Yes.\n\nPerson responsible: \"Mannie Panesar – Head of Technical Services\" → person_responsible \"Mannie Panesar\", person_title \"Head of Technical Services\".\n\nNow description text — compose:\n\n\"Self-indicating, single-interval, non-automatic weighing instruments for paint mixing applications; not to be used for direct sales to the public. Family comprises the PMA-EV (Class II) and PMA-HD (Class III) types. Construction: plastic housing, stainless steel load receptor, plastic display head, 3 feet (2 adjustable) with level indicator. Display: LCD segment display with fixed position touch keys (models EVO1*, LAB1*, SPO1*) or LCD graphic display with touch function (models VIS1*). EX-link converter for voltage supply and data transfer on models EVO1X*, LAB1X*, VIS1X*, VIS1Y*. Devices: initial zero-setting device (≤ 20% of Max), zero-tracking device (≤ 4% of Max), zero indicator (models LAB1*, SPO1*), combined zero-setting and tare-balancing device, preset tare device (only via a PC), approximate displaying device (models EVO1*, VIS1*), weight accumulation, weight calculating. Interfaces: USB either directly (models SPO1S*, EVO1S*, EVO1Y*) or via Ex-link converter (models EVO1X*, LAB1X*); Ethernet via Ex-link converter (models VIS1X*, VIS1Y*). A PC with compatible paint mixing software can be connected via the interfaces provided; all devices can be initiated via a PC, preset-tare only via a PC. One strain gauge load cell manufactured by Bizerba. Power supply 5 V DC from USB; for models LAB1X*, EVO1X*, VIS1X*, VIS1Y* power is delivered from USB to the Ex-link converter and then to the instrument via RJ-45. Sealing: access to electronics and load cell prevented by two tamper-evident labels on the underside; menu access switch covered by a tamper-evident label.\"\n\nNote: the \"≤\" character is fine in JSON (not LaTeX).\n\nNaming convention note: \"A = S, X, Y denotes standard / Ex-zone 1 / Ex-zone 2 environments; B = 1–4 denotes supplied RJ-45 cable length (none, 10 m, 20 m, 30 m; B = 1 for A = S and EVO1Y models); C is a placeholder for commercial name affixes.\" Include in description. Good.\n\nNow let me write the full JSON. I need to be extremely careful with syntax validity. I'll avoid comments and trailing commas.\n\nLet me build the model_level arrays carefully. Attribute: accuracy_class — values: 28 \"II\" then 10 \"III\".\n\nActually hold on, one consideration: is it necessary to include accuracy_class AND display_type in model_level given they're also implied? I've decided yes for accuracy_class, and display_type... let me include it. Fine.\n\nTotal JSON size estimate: models 38 lines × ~2 lines each (I can fit each model entry on one line) = ~38 long lines. model_level: 7 attributes, each with header + 38 one-line values = ~280 lines. Total ~350 lines + rest ~80 lines. Acceptable.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nAlso note: for values arrays in model_level, keep order consistent (EV 28 then HD 10).\n\nFor operating_temperature_range: values {\"model\": ..., \"value\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 30}}.\n\nUnit symbol \"°C\" — Unicode fine.\n\nWrite the JSON now.\n\nCareful: \"Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG\" — ampersand fine in JSON.\n\nOne more: certificate-level \"scheme\": \"A\".\n\ncertified_type category — schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\". For weighing instrument: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Good.\n\nmodule_designation: