umerate type_designations: [\"PMA-EV\", \"PMA-HD\", \"EVO1S1N3CI-C\", \"EVO1S1CI-C\", \"EVO1Y1N3CI-C\", \"EVO1Y1CI-C\", \"EVO1X1N3CI-C\", \"EVO1X1CI-C\", \"EVO1X2N3CI-C\", \"EVO1X2CI-C\", \"EVO1X3N3CI-C\", \"EVO1X3CI-C\", \"EVO1X4N3CI-C\", \"EVO1X4CI-C\", \"VIS1Y1M3CI-C\", \"VIS1Y1CI-C\", \"VIS1Y2M3CI-C\", \"VIS1Y2CI-C\", \"VIS1Y3M3CI-C\", \"VIS1Y3CI-C\", \"VIS1Y4M3CI-C\", \"VIS1Y4CI-C\", \"VIS1X1M3CI-C\", \"VIS1X1CI-C\", \"VIS1X2M3CI-C\", \"VIS1X2CI-C\", \"VIS1X3M3CI-C\", \"VIS1X3CI-C\", \"VIS1X4M3CI-C\", \"VIS1X4CI-C\", \"SPO1S1N3CI-C\", \"SPO1S1CI-C\", \"LAB1X1N3CI-C\", \"LAB1X1CI-C\", \"LAB1X2N3CI-C\", \"LAB1X2CI-C\", \"LAB1X3N3CI-C\", \"LAB1X3CI-C\", \"LAB1X4N3CI-C\", \"LAB1X4CI-C\"]\n\nNow writing the answer. I'll produce compact but valid JSON. Given the repetition, I'll put each model value object on its own line in values arrays.\n\nLet me write out the model_level attribute blocks.\n\nattribute: accuracy_class, unit_symbol: null\nvalues: 28 × {\"model\": \"<EV model>\", \"value\": \"II\"} then 10 × {\"model\": \"<HD model>\", \"value\": \"III\"}\n\nattribute: maximum_capacity, unit_symbol: \"g\"\n28 × 7500, 10 × 2200\n\nattribute: minimum_capacity, unit_symbol: \"g\"\n28 × 5, 10 × 20\n\nattribute: actual_scale_interval, unit_symbol: \"g\"\n28 × 0.1, 10 × 1\n\nattribute: operating_temperature_range, unit_symbol: \"°C\"\n28 × {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 30}, 10 × {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 40}\n\nattribute: load_cell, unit_symbol: null\n28 × \"Bizerba WS8\", 10 × \"Bizerba WS3\"\n\nattribute: display_type, unit_symbol: null\n- \"LCD segment display with fixed position touch keys\" for EVO1 (12), LAB1 (8), SPO1 (2) = 22\n- \"LCD graphic display with touch function\" for VIS1 (16)\n\nOrder for display_type: keep the same model order: EVO1 (12 segment), VIS1 (16 graphic), SPO1+LAB1 (10 segment).\n\nOK. Let me now also double check the initial zero setting: \"Initial zero setting device (≤ 20% of Max)\". Attribute initial_zero_setting_range {min:null, max:20} unit \"%\". Reference: \"Max\". I'll name second attribute \"initial_zero_setting_reference\".\n\nzero tracking: \"Zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max)\" → zero_tracking_range {min:null, max:4} \"%\", zero_tracking_reference \"Max\".\n\nmaximum_tare: type_level {\"value\": \"-Max\"} — hmm, maybe better \"maximum_tare\" with value \"Max\" and unit null; the \"-\" in the table might be a placeholder dash. Looking at row: `<td>T ≤</td><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">- Max</td>`. Probably intended \"T ≤ Max\" with a dash prefix meaning \"up to −Max\"? In R76 certificates, subtractive tare range often shown as \"− Max … + Max\"? Hmm. Or \"T ≤ - Max\" meaning tare capacity up to Max. I'll record value \"-Max\" verbatim. Hmm... Actually I think recording verbatim is safest: value \"-Max\", unit null. Yes.\n\nNow let me also think — the software table: \"Weighing instrument software BAC | 05496 1578 (VIS models) | 00-56-02.xx\". And separate table \"Software applications APC (only VIS models) | 6587 | 01-72-03.xx\". Good as drafted.\n\nAlso add sealing note maybe to description. Already included.\n\nWrite final JSON. Check decimal: \"0.1\" as number 0.1. \"1\" as 1. Values 7500, 2200, 5, 20, 8, 3.\n\nAlso dates: revision date \"11/05/2018\" → normalize \"2018-05-11\".\n\nTest report date \"11 May 2018\" → \"2018-05-11\".\n\nI think we're ready. One last thought on \"label\" in models[]: I'll set label to \"PMA-EV\" / \"PMA-HD\" (the type). Good.\n\nAlso environment values: \"standard\", \"Ex-zone 1\", \"Ex-zone 2\".\n\nLet me write it all out now, carefully.\n\nActually one more consideration about components: role naming — use readable snake: \"load_cell\", \"ex_link_converter\", \"weighing_instrument_software\", \"software_application\". Fine.\n\nFor the ex_link_converter component with empty type_designations — fine.\n\nFinal JSON below. Ensure no trailing commas, proper quoting.",
